        add("guide.orbitalis.fuels.title", "Fuels");
        add("guide.orbitalis.fuels.text", "A rocket engine burns a fuel with an oxidizer, and together they're called propellants. You have three to start with.\n\nRP-1 kerosene, a fuel that's easy to store.\nLiquid oxygen, the oxidizer.\nLiquid hydrogen, very efficient but hard to keep.\n\nFluids are measured in mB, and a bucket is 1,000 mB.\n\nThe refinery makes RP-1. It's crafted from 5 steel plates, 1 heating element, 2 copper ingots and 1 redstone.\n\nPut coal in the blue slot. Each coal gives 250 mB of RP-1 in 5 seconds, for 4,000 FE.\n\nLike every fluid machine, it pushes its output into the blocks touching it. Just place a tank against it.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.cryogenics.title", "Oxygen and Hydrogen");
        add("guide.orbitalis.cryogenics.text", "Liquid oxygen and hydrogen are made in two steps.\n\n1. The electrolyzer splits water into two gases. It's crafted from 4 steel plates, 1 glass, 2 copper ingots, 1 bucket and 1 redstone.\n\nPlaced against a water source, it fills itself. You can also empty a bucket into it. 100 mB of water gives 200 mB of hydrogen and 100 mB of oxygen, in 1 second, for 1,600 FE.\n\n2. The cryogenic liquefier cools a gas until it turns liquid. It's crafted from 5 aluminum plates, 1 electric motor, 2 packed ice and 1 redstone.\n\n100 mB of gas gives 50 mB of liquid in 2 seconds. That's 2,000 FE for oxygen and 3,200 FE for hydrogen.\n\nFacing the electrolyzer, hydrogen comes out on the left (red mark) and oxygen on the right (blue mark). So you need a liquefier on each side, or a pipe leading to one.\n\nDon't forget to power every machine, with a generator touching it or a cable.\n\nGases travel in gas pipes and liquids in liquid pipes. Between the electrolyzer and the liquefiers you need gas pipes, and after them, liquid pipes.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.storage.title", "Storage");
        add("guide.orbitalis.storage.text", "The cryogenic tank holds 32,000 mB of a single fluid. It's crafted from 6 aluminum plates, 2 insulation blankets and 1 glass.\n\nPlaced against the machine that makes the fluid, it fills itself.\n\nLiquid oxygen and hydrogen are cryogenic, they have to stay very cold. As long as it's powered, the tank keeps them without loss for 10 FE per tick, and its screen reads \"Active cooling\".\n\nWithout power, it reads \"Boiling off!\" and loses 5 mB per second.\n\nRP-1 and water don't need power. The screen then reads \"Stable storage\".\n\nHover over a gauge to see the fluid and the amount.\n\nThe modular tank is simpler. 6 steel plates and 2 glass make 2 blocks of 16,000 mB. Placed together, they merge into one square tank, from a single block up to 3×3 and 12 high, which is 1,728,000 mB. You can see the level through its windows, and right-clicking with an empty hand tells you what's inside.\n\nIt takes any liquid or gas, but only one at a time. Two tanks holding different fluids stay separate even when they touch. If you remove a block, the contents stay in the others, as long as there's room.\n\nIt isn't cooled, so a cryogenic propellant boils off at 2.5 mB per second per block. Think of it as a buffer, and keep the cryogenic tank for oxygen and hydrogen.\n\nIt doesn't push anything by itself. Empty it with a pipe in extraction mode (see \"Cables and Pipes\") or with a bucket.\n\nA control screen placed against a tank, or linked with a data cable, shows its level. The same screen linked to a launch console shows the launcher and its pad, and linked to a tracking station, the satellites. Panels merge into one screen, up to 16×9. Sneak and right-click to switch to the next display.");

