        add("guide.orbitalis.conduits.title", "Cables and Pipes");
        add("guide.orbitalis.conduits.text", "Conduits link your machines at a distance, so they no longer need to touch. There are four of them.\n\nThe power cable is a dark sheath with copper collars. 2 copper ingots with 1 redstone in the middle, in a row, make 6 cables. Its strands light up and pulse when current flows.\n\nThe liquid pipe is a thick glass tube in a steel cage. 2 steel plates with 1 glass block in the middle, in a row, make 6 pipes. Stained glass works too. It carries water, RP-1 and liquid propellants, and you can see the liquid flowing inside.\n\nThe gas pipe is a thin glass tube with brass rings. 2 glass blocks with 1 copper ingot in the middle, in a row, make 6 pipes. Stained glass works too. It carries gaseous hydrogen and oxygen between the electrolyzer and the liquefiers. Hydrogen swirls through it in red, oxygen in blue.\n\nLay them end to end and they connect on their own, to each other and to machines. Two different kinds of conduit won't join, and a pipe refuses what isn't meant for it. Gas won't go into a liquid pipe.\n\nConduits store nothing. Whatever a machine pushes in arrives at the other end straight away.\n\nPower is shared equally between the machines on a network, and several generators can feed the same network.\n\nFor fluids, the first block that accepts is filled before the next. An empty tank takes any liquid, so give each liquid its own network if you're filling several tanks. Going to the launch pad, though, a single pipe is enough for every propellant.\n\nAgainst a machine, the tube ends in a fitting and a collar in its colour. A long straight run has no joints and reads as a single tube.\n\nA machine pushes what it makes, but a tank pushes nothing. To empty one, sneak and right-click the pipe touching it with an empty hand. The collar turns orange and striped, and the pipe pulls 2,000 mB per second into the rest of the network. In this mode it delivers nothing to the blocks it touches. Do the same again to switch it back.\n\nThe data cable is a thin black cable with turquoise dots. 2 aluminum plates and 1 redstone make 4 cables. It carries neither power nor fluid. It links control screens to what they display: launch consoles, tracking stations, tanks.");

