        add("guide.orbitalis.section.rockets", "ROCKETS");
        add("guide.orbitalis.rocket_stats.title", "Statistics");
        add("guide.orbitalis.rocket_stats.text", "The design computer recalculates everything as soon as you change a setting.\n\nMass is the whole rocket, tanks full.\n\nThrust is the first stage's thrust at liftoff.\n\nTWR is thrust divided by weight. It must be above 1 at liftoff, or the rocket won't leave the ground. Add engines or remove tanks.\n\nDelta-v is all the speed the rocket can gain. It comes from the Tsiolkovsky equation, specific impulse × 9.81 × ln(full mass / empty mass). To raise it, use a more efficient engine, lighter tanks, or add a stage.\n\nAs a rough guide, you need about 1,700 m/s to reach space, 9,400 m/s for low orbit and 11,850 m/s for a geostationary transfer.\n\nFor the same volume, a tank holds almost three times less hydrogen than RP-1. The hydrogen engine is more efficient, but it needs more tanks.\n\nThe last line says what the rocket can reach, or the first problem to fix. Hover over the screen for each stage's details: propellant, dry mass and burn time.\n\nAt launch, the flight is worked out step by step from these numbers. The rocket feels its thrust, its weight and air drag. It gets lighter as it burns propellant, drops its empty stages, and an orbital launcher gradually tips over toward the horizontal. Liquid-fuel engines throttle down so as not to exceed 4 g. The screens show the flight sped up (altitude, speed, acceleration, active stage) with the mission clock in real time. Whatever propellant is left at orbit insertion is your margin.\n\nThe ion thruster (\"Ion Propulsion\" technology) and the solar sail (\"Solar Sails\" technology) are placed like engines on the last stage, without tanks. The thruster's xenon is loaded at the factory, and the sail needs no propellant at all. Their thrust is tiny. They can't lift off and only work in orbit, where they accelerate for months. So their delta-v only counts if the chemical stages reach low orbit on their own, and is then added to the rest. The ion thruster has a specific impulse of 3,100 s, ten times a chemical engine. The sail gives a fixed impulse, so the lighter the payload, the more delta-v. Neither is suitable for a crew, the trip would take months.\n\nThe nuclear thermal engine (\"Nuclear Thermal Propulsion\" technology) heats liquid hydrogen in its reactor, with no oxygen. At 900 s of specific impulse it does twice as well as the best chemical engine, and its vacuum thrust is decent. But it weighs almost four tonnes, and hydrogen alone is so light that it needs a lot of tanks. It's only for upper stages. A blueprint that puts it on the first stage is refused, since its reactor never lights above the pad. It only needs hydrogen when fuelling.");

