        add("guide.orbitalis.engineering.title", "The Engineer's Workbench");
        add("guide.orbitalis.engineering.text", "The skill tree gives your agency technologies, which is to say knowledge. The engineer's workbench turns that into hardware.\n\nIt's a drafting table two blocks by two, one block high. It's crafted from three steel plates, two iron ingots on each side and a crafting table in the middle. Right-click it to open its four sections: engines, tanks, payloads and launchers.\n\nTo study a part, you need its technology, researched in the tree, and the materials for a prototype, taken from your inventory. Those are mostly plates of the metal of the day, copper and redstone. The sheet compares what you have with what's needed. There's no luck involved. As soon as everything is there, the study succeeds, once and for all, for the whole agency.\n\nA studied part can be used, both on a printed blueprint and on a rocket built by hand. Studying a launcher (its engines and tanks must already be studied) lets you print it ready-made at the design computer. So you can invent your own rockets from the parts alone, and only study a launcher to save time.\n\nA study's dot is green when done, blue if it's possible now, orange if materials are missing, and grey if the technology or parts are missing.");
        add("design.orbitalis.nothing_studied", "Nothing to design yet. Head to the engineer's workbench first and study an engine and a tank. Then you can put rockets together here, stage by stage, or start from a ready-made model.");
