        add("guide.orbitalis.launch.title", "Launch");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch.text", "1. Build. Print the rocket's blueprint at the design computer, put it on the table of a working pad, then place the blocks it shows. Right-clicking the deck with no block in hand places a whole layer. The rocket forms with the last block.\n\n2. Set up. Right-clicking the table with the rocket stands it up on the pad. The pad must be formed, big enough for its mass, and its tower taller than the rocket. To take it back, sneak and right-click the table with an empty hand, or use the recovery remote within 48 blocks.\n\n3. The launch sequence. It's shown on the left of the mission control screen, and on board, the jump key opens it. Its steps run in order, one click each. Nothing is left to chance. Each thing you forget always has the same consequence.\n\n- Fill valves. Open them and fuelling runs from the tanks hooked up at the foot of the tower, RP-1 first, then the cold propellants (1 mB per 10 kg). The gauge takes at least fifteen seconds or so to fill. At 100%, click again to close the valves, or the pressure builds up. Without a full load, the engines will cut out in flight. A solid rocket doesn't need this.\n- Pressurisation. It lasts two minutes and stops the cold propellants from boiling off. Without it nothing lights, and if it drops, part of the cold propellant goes with it.\n- Arms and walkway. Retract them. Once the arm is retracted, nothing more goes into the rocket and nobody can board. If you forget them, the rocket rips them off as it leaves and tears itself open.\n- Water deluge. It uses the pad's water reserve, filled through the water fuel port in a corner at the foot of the tower, with a pipe or buckets. Without the deluge, the flames break deck slabs.\n- Ignition. Thrust builds up over three seconds.\n- Release the clamps, only at full thrust. Too early and the rocket falls back onto its table. The clamps hold for twelve seconds, after which the engines are cut and part of the propellant is lost.\n\nWhile the rocket is held down, \"Cut the engines\" stops everything.\n\nThe sequence carries on in flight. Its list shows the steps of this flight, and each lights up when its moment comes. You have at least five seconds to command it.\n\nThe flight. The rocket follows its trajectory on its own. On board, you don't fly it, you make the decisions. Each one appears in the middle of the screen with its key (the space bar by default, changeable in the options under \"Orbitalis: piloting\") and a draining gauge. Press before it runs out.\n\nTwo or three times per flight, at random, a gust or a drifting nozzle pushes the rocket off course. The screen then asks you to hold a key for a few seconds, or to press it in time. If you make it, the rocket gets back on course. If not, it veers off (the stall alert sounds), loses speed and, in dense air, strains its structure, before the computer straightens it out.\n\nA flight lasts 20 to 50 seconds depending on the destination and the number of stages. Between decisions, the screen shows the next one and how long until it comes. On the left, a timeline shows the climb and the flight's steps, and a line at the bottom gives speed, altitude, apoapsis and stage. At liftoff, the view switches outside and slowly circles the rocket as long as you leave the mouse alone. M opens the orbit map. An uncrewed rocket is commanded the same way from the console.\n\nThe flight computer can help. Click \"Assist\", at the top right of the screen, to change its level (only on the ground). In assisted mode (\"Gyroscopes\" technology), it throttles down and cuts the engines itself. In automatic mode (\"Flight Computers\"), it does every flight step and closes the valves once fuelling is done. The ground sequence is still yours. Each flight step done by hand earns a 2 research point bonus.\n- Throttle down (liquid fuel), between the 3rd and 8th second. Otherwise the structure fails at max Q.\n- Separate the stage, at each empty stage. Otherwise nothing drops it, the rocket drags it along with engines out, and it's lost.\n- Jettison the fairing (uncrewed), once in space. Otherwise the payload stays shut in and the mission fails.\n- Cut the engines (orbital flights), in the last five seconds. Otherwise the orbit is missed and the mission fails.\n\nA missed step can't be made up, and a flight missing one earns nothing. Only the flight computer can do them for you, at the assist level you picked.\n\n4. Follow the flight. The console, the screen walls and the cockpit screen show altitude and speed. At the end, the agency gets its research points. There's a bonus the first time it reaches a milestone (10 for an atmospheric flight, 30 for space, 100 for low orbit...), and 5 points per sounding rocket payload sent to space. A satellite goes into orbit, a station module deploys, a capsule comes back down.\n\nThe launcher itself is lost after the flight.\n\nAccidents. A lost rocket doesn't just vanish. It breaks up section by section, each according to what it holds: a fireball for kerosene, a white cloud and a flash for cold propellants, a shower of sparks for solid fuel. The shockwave hurts and throws back anything close. The nearest pad blocks are destroyed and the blueprint shows what to rebuild, but the table and the deflector always hold. Around it, the ground is scorched, a few fires start and debris rains down. Pick it up, it smelts back into steel. The damage depends on the rocket's mass and propellant. A sounding rocket chips a few slabs, a fully loaded heavy launcher flattens the deck and the foot of the tower. A rocket lost in flight explodes in the air, and its wreck lands further away, along the trench. The server option \"Rocket accident damage\" can limit this damage to the pad, or turn it off.\n\nThe pace. Each operation takes a few seconds (the valves open, pressure rises, the arm swings away), and nothing else can be commanded meanwhile. Before ignition, running a poll is a good idea. Each station answers go or no-go, and if any says no-go, ignition is held. The answer is valid for 90 seconds. Nothing forces you. Without a poll, the ignition button always works, even if fuelling isn't done, and the rocket will blow up in flight when its engines cut out.\n\nBy technology. A solid rocket has no fuelling and no pressurisation. A turbopump engine (kerosene) also needs chilling through the arm, which only lasts two minutes. A staged-combustion or hydrogen launcher must also switch to internal power. The list only shows the steps your rocket needs.\n\nOverpressure. Once fuelling is done, close the valves. If they stay open while the pad's tanks keep pushing, pressure builds up in the rocket. The relief valves vent, the valve line flashes red, then the alarm goes off. After thirty seconds, the tanks burst and the rocket explodes on the pad. Once the valves are closed, pressure drops in a few seconds. In automatic mode, the flight computer closes them itself.\n\nPad tests. In the \"Go / No-Go\" window of mission control, you can run three tests whenever you like, with or without a rocket, as long as nothing is lit. The deluge runs for eight seconds and uses a quarter of a real deluge's water, the arms swing out and back, and the alarm sounds. A test deluge doesn't protect a real liftoff.\n\nMission control. Its screen brings everything together. On the left, the launch sequence, which you can run from here. On the right, a 3D model of the pad, animated like the world: the arms swing away, the engines light, the rocket rises. Rotate it with the mouse and zoom with the scroll wheel. A tab switches to the navigation map, which the screen also does by itself at liftoff. Below, the telemetry. The \"Go / No-Go\" button opens a window with the stations, the poll and the pad tests. \"Abort launch\" cuts everything while the rocket is held down.\n\nThe navigation map. The console and the screens show the terrain in blocks, seen at three-quarter view, like the atlas. In flight, the map pulls back as the rocket climbs, until it shows the whole planet. The rocket follows its trajectory. It climbs straight, gradually tips toward its heading and races for the horizon, and an orbital launcher then circles the globe. Behind it you see the path flown and its shadow on the ground, ahead, the planned path in dashes with the stage separations, up to orbit insertion. The \"View\" button in a corner turns the map a quarter turn.\n\nThe atlas. The \"Atlas\" button, at the top of the mission control map, opens the world map full screen. Drag to move around, and zoom with the scroll wheel, from single blocks to a whole region. The \"View\" button at the bottom changes the angle, and a compass at the top right points north. From afar, each pad is drawn as a miniature. The cursor gives coordinates, altitude and terrain type, and markers show mission control, the pads and your position. Wherever someone has already been, the map shows the world as it is, buildings and trees included, even if the area is no longer loaded. Where nobody has gone, it shows the terrain the world will generate. A mission control only knows its surroundings, whose limit is dotted.\n\nLayers. The \"Layers\" button at the bottom shows or hides three layers. Facilities shows the pads and their state (free, rocket and propellants, countdown, in flight), mission controls and tracking stations, even far away and unloaded, with their last known state. Flights in progress shows the ground track as a solid line, then its continuation in dashes, with altitude and distance flown. Danger zones shows, around a rocket on its pad, where the shockwave and debris would reach if it blew up, and under a rocket in flight, the corridor where its pieces would fall. Click a facility or the terrain to open its card, and the cross closes it.\n\nThe sky. Two more layers depend on your agency's satellites. With a weather satellite in service, the map shows the world's real clouds from above, with their shadows, grey and rainy when it rains. They fade as you zoom in. Around tracking stations, a green square shows the ground surveyed by observation satellites. Until the navigation constellation has four satellites, your position is only known to within a few blocks, and an orange circle shows it. At the bottom right, a line sums up the sky (weather and relay). Click it to expand weather, relay, navigation and observation.\n\nThe mission tablet. With the \"Mission Tablet\" technology, the tablet (6 aluminum plates, a display, a circuit board and a redstone) opens the atlas with a right-click, anywhere on the planet, as long as one of your agency's telecommunications satellites relays the data. The atlas is then centred on you, with a \"via satellite\" badge. Without a relay, or away from the planet, the tablet tells you so.\n\nWhat the agency knows. On its own, a mission control knows 1,536 blocks around it. Each observation satellite in service adds 1,280 blocks, up to 4,096.\n\nMarkers. Right-click the map to place a marker your whole agency can see. Its card says where it is and who placed it. You can rename it (Enter to confirm, Escape to cancel) or remove it.");

