        add("guide.orbitalis.section.orbit", "ORBIT");
        add("guide.orbitalis.satellites.title", "Satellites");
        add("guide.orbitalis.satellites.text", "A satellite is a payload. At the design computer, it takes the place of the sounding rocket's instruments, under the fairing. If the flight succeeds, it goes into orbit and works for your agency.\n\nEach satellite aims for a specific orbit, which the launcher must be able to reach:\n- low orbit (LEO, 400 km) and polar orbit (SSO, 800 km): 9,400 m/s of delta-v;\n- medium orbit (MEO, 20,200 km) and geostationary (GEO, 35,786 km): 11,850 m/s. The launcher drops the satellite on a transfer orbit and its own engine does the rest, just like for real.\nIf the launcher falls short, the satellite is lost. The rocket's tooltip and the launch table warn you beforehand.\n\nThe satellites:\n- Experimental satellite (85 kg, LEO): 1 research point a day for 15 days.\n- Weather satellite (700 kg, SSO): 2 points a day and weather forecasts, for 60 days.\n- Observation satellite (1,200 kg, SSO): 4 points a day for 60 days.\n- Telecommunications satellite (1,500 kg, GEO): relays the others' data continuously, for 120 days.\n- Navigation satellite (1,000 kg, MEO): 1 point a day for 100 days. Four in service form a constellation, which earns a bonus.\n- Space telescope (11 t, LEO): 12 points a day for 150 days.\n\nThe first satellite of each type earns a bonus. Watch the mass, since the heavier the payload, the less delta-v the launcher has. At the end of its life, a satellite stops working and must be replaced. Lifespans can be turned off in the server settings.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.tracking_station.title", "The Tracking Station");
        add("guide.orbitalis.tracking_station.text", "The tracking station is a dish antenna. Place it under open sky and right-click it. The map shows the planet, the four orbits and your satellites, each going round at its own pace.\n\nA science satellite produces data continuously and keeps it in memory for three days at most. Beyond that, the oldest is lost. The \"Receive data\" button turns it into research points, so drop by regularly.\n\nWith a telecommunications satellite in service, data comes in on its own, wherever you are.\n\nWith a weather satellite, the station shows the current weather and how long it will last. That's handy for timing a launch, since you can't launch in a thunderstorm.\n\nSatellites belong to your agency. All your stations show the same thing, and your teammates see them too.\n\nThe console benefits as well. With a weather satellite, it shows the launch window and warns you when the weather turns. With a telecommunications satellite, it controls a pad up to 256 blocks away instead of 64.\n\nThe \"Survey\" tab shows what your observation satellites see, the ground around the station chunk by chunk, with its ores. One satellite covers a square five chunks wide, two cover seven, and three or more cover nine. Pick an ore in the list to show only that one, and hover over a square for its coordinates.\n\nThe \"Observatory\" button turns your satellites to the sky. With the \"Tracking Stations\" technology, pick a body and point your instruments at it. Each observation satellite counts for one, each space telescope for three, and only the telescope sees deep sky (nebulae, galaxies, the black hole). Each body is only visible at certain times, which come round again over the days: the Moon around full moon, Mars at opposition, a comet as it passes, deep sky in its season. Outside of that, the instruments stay pointed and wait, and the screen says when the window opens. The image builds up as you observe. Once the body is discovered, receive its image (you need to see the sky, or have a relay). It earns research points and the body goes into the catalogue.\n\nEach discovery leads to others. The Moon leads to Mars and Venus, Mars to Jupiter, the Pleiades to deep sky. The catalogue collects your discoveries, a double page per body with its photo and its card. For a planet or moon, the card explains how to get there (distance, travel time, delta-v, gravity, atmosphere, temperature), which launcher to take, and what equipment to bring, technology by technology, showing what your agency already has.");

