        add("entity.orbitalis.capsule", "Crew Capsule");
        add("creative.orbitalis.rocket.crewed_suborbital", "Crewed Suborbital Rocket (test)");
        add("creative.orbitalis.rocket.crewed_orbital", "Crewed Orbital Launcher (test)");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.boarded", "On board. While the rocket is on the ground, sneak to climb back down.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.no_capsule", "This rocket carries no crew capsule.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.too_late", "Too late to board: the engines are lit.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.occupied", "The capsule is full: all three seats are taken.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.abort", "LAUNCHER FAILURE: escape tower fired. Arm the parachutes!");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.apogee", "Engine cutoff: you are in space");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.orbit_insertion", "Orbit insertion complete");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.reentry", "Falling back: atmospheric reentry");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.deorbit", "Deorbit burn: atmospheric reentry");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.chutes", "Parachutes deployed");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.landed", "Capsule down, the crew is fine. Sneak to get out, then sneak and right-click the capsule to pick it up.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.first_suborbital", "First crewed flight to space: +%s research points.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.first_orbital", "First crewed orbital flight: +%s research points.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.hud.apogee", "Apogee: weightlessness");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.hud.orbit", "In orbit: lap %s/%s");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.hud.reentry", "Atmospheric reentry: %s m above ground");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.hud.chute", "Under parachutes: %s m above ground");
        add("guide.orbitalis.section.crew", "CREWED FLIGHT");
        add("guide.orbitalis.crewed_flight.title", "Crewed Flight");
        add("guide.orbitalis.crewed_flight.text", "The crew capsule is a 2,800 kg payload that goes under the fairing at the design computer. It needs the \"Crew Capsules\" technology and includes the cabin, the heat shield, the parachutes and the escape tower.\n\nOnce the rocket is on the table, right-click the cabin to climb aboard. Right-clicking anywhere else on the rocket opens the pad screen. When you get out, you end up on the boarding gate, back to the rocket. As long as it's on the ground, sneak to climb down.\n\nOn board, the launch key (Jump by default) opens the launch sequence, the same as at the console. You run it yourself, from fuelling to clamp release. The first to climb in is the pilot, seated up front, and only they have the controls. Two crew members can sit behind. The cockpit screen is on the ceiling and shows the mission clock, altitude, speed, trajectory and system status. The windows are on the sides.\n\nThe capsule rises with the rocket. What happens next depends on the launcher:\n- on an atmospheric flight, it falls straight back;\n- on a suborbital flight, you get a few seconds of weightlessness at the top;\n- on an orbital flight (9,400 m/s of delta-v), you stay in orbit until you decide to come home (see \"Space\").\nThen comes re-entry. Below 450 m, the air has slowed the capsule enough to arm the parachutes (Jump by default). They then open on their own at 140 m. Without them, the capsule crashes. In automatic mode, the flight computer arms them for you. You can't leave the capsule in flight.\n\nIf the launcher fails, the escape tower pulls the capsule off the rocket. Again, arming the parachutes is up to you, and quickly if it happens close to the ground.\n\nOn the ground, sneak to get out. Sneak and right-click the landed capsule to pick it up, it's reusable. The first crewed flight to space earns 150 research points, the first one to orbit 300, as long as you come back.\n\nAn uncrewed rocket can also carry a capsule. It's a test flight, and it comes back the same way.\n\nThere are three capsules. The Mk1 \"Scout\" has three seats, a dial panel and a navigation globe (\"Crew Capsules\" technology, 2.8 t). The Mk2 \"Horizon\" has four seats, three big screens and a touch console, with no stick (\"Rendezvous and Docking\" technology, 3.6 t). The Mk3 \"Odyssey\" has four seats, three screens, a big ceiling panel and a stick (\"Lunar Lander\" technology, 4.5 t). The fourth crew member sits in the middle, behind the pilot, on a step.");

