        add("guide.orbitalis.facilities.title", "Space Facilities");
        add("guide.orbitalis.facilities.text", "The world hides space facilities. There are weather stations, research centres, observation centres and, much more rarely, control centres with their own launch pad. They show up in plains, savanna, desert, badlands, snow or on beaches, away from villages and behind a fence.\n\nA weather station has an observation building, an anemometer mast and an instrument field. The bigger ones also have a radar tower under a radome and a shelter for weather balloons.\n\nA research centre is a two-storey lab. The bigger ones have an engine test stand and a hangar.\n\nAn observation centre has a telescope dome, its control room and a small antenna. The bigger ones have a radio telescope.\n\nA control centre has its firing room facing the pad, the assembly building with a rocket inside, a water tower, and the pad with its service tower.\n\nAbout one in three is abandoned. The concrete is cracked, the cladding rusted, the windows broken, and the chests have already been looted.\n\nThe original equipment no longer works. Exhibit rocket parts, slabs, deflector, tower, consoles, screens, solar panels or floodlights only give back a bit of scrap when taken apart. Anything you place there yourself comes back normally.\n\nChests hold plates, ingots, rocket parts, sometimes a satellite, a spacesuit or a launch pad kit. You'll often find an archive blueprint, on a drafting table or in a chest, describing a ready-made rocket. Before building it, have the blueprint validated at the engineer's workbench, in the Archives tab. You need the technologies of its parts and half of its prototype. The launcher is then studied, and you earn research points along the way.\n\nThe blocks of these buildings can be crafted too: formwork concrete (with its slab and stairs), technical tiles, safety stripes, white, blue or rusted metal cladding, louvres, radome panels, curtain-wall glazing and antenna mesh.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.space.title", "Space");
        add("guide.orbitalis.space.text", "A crewed orbital flight really takes you to space. Once the engines cut off, the capsule and its crew are in orbit, in vacuum, under a black sky.\n\nIn orbit there's no up or down. You float and move as if in water, swimming where you look, then drifting and slowly stopping. Jump to go up, sneak to go down, sprint to swim faster.\n\nThe capsule is pressurised, you can breathe normally inside. To get out (sneak), you need a complete spacesuit: helmet, chest, leggings and boots. If a piece is missing, it isn't airtight. The chest carries the life support pack, with ten minutes of oxygen, shown on the item like a durability bar. The helmet shows the remaining oxygen, the pressure and the seal on the left of the visor. Without a suit, or with an empty tank, you suffocate in a few seconds.\n\nThe advanced suit, white, is made of titanium around the pieces of the orange suit. It lasts a quarter of an hour and protects better, but above all, each piece takes modules. Right-click with a module to fit it to the piece you're wearing, or holding in your other hand, and sneak-right-click with the piece in hand to remove the last one fitted. The helmet can take the proximity map and the environment sensor, shown on the right of the visor. The chest takes an extra oxygen tank (ten more minutes) and thrusters for weightlessness. The leggings take shock absorbers.\n\nThe suit recharges while you sit in the capsule, and on its own once you're back on the planet.\n\nTo come home, right-click the capsule to get back in, then press the return key (Jump by default). The flight computer plans the braking burn and starts a countdown. At the mark, hold the key and let go between 95 and 110% of the requested delta-v. The capsule then re-enters above its pad and lands under its parachutes. Don't lose sight of it, it's your only way home.\n\nYour agency's first spacewalk earns 100 research points. Without a crew, an orbital flight is just a test flight, and the capsule comes back on its own after two orbits.");

