        add("guide.orbitalis.stations.text", "A station starts with its core module, a 3 t payload (\"Station Modules\" technology) launched uncrewed. If the launcher reaches low orbit, the module deploys on its own right above the pad. It's a padded, pressurised room three blocks across, with three windows, the docking port, the air generator on the ceiling, the terminal under the north window, a half-charged battery, a wing of five solar panels on the roof and an antenna.\n\nTo get there, launch a crewed orbital flight from the same site. The capsule arrives about thirty blocks away, lines up with the port, then closes in by itself, slowing down until contact. Stay on board during the manoeuvre. Once docked, sneak to go through the port, and you're inside, no suit needed. To leave, right-click the port from inside, then hold the jump key.\n\nRight-clicking the terminal opens the station dashboard. It shows the layout, power (production, consumption, batteries), orbit, air, lab, greenhouse, crew, cargo and ships. The station goes around the planet in five minutes, a little over a third of that in shadow, and the header counts down the eclipse.\n\nThe air generator pressurises the whole enclosed volume around it, up to 4,000 m³, as long as it gets 20 FE per tick. A broken wall or a port open to space, and the air is gone in two seconds. Keep a suit within reach. Greenhouse plants recycle the air, and each planted tray saves the generator 2 FE per tick.\n\nEverything in the station shares the same power grid. A solar panel (\"Solar Panels\" technology) gives 40 FE per tick in sunlight and nothing during eclipse. Whatever its neighbours don't use goes into the batteries (500,000 FE each), which everything else draws from. Plan enough to last through the eclipse.\n\nEach module is crafted with its equipment, then taken on board, in your inventory or as launcher cargo, in which case it's delivered behind the port. Right-click a wall from inside to deploy it. The module is built on the other side, fully fitted, and the passage opens.\n• Habitation: four bunks, four lockers and a terminal. An injured crew member rests on a bunk with a right-click and recovers.\n• Laboratory: eight experiment racks. In orbit, with air and power (40 FE per tick), each rack runs one experiment per orbit, every five minutes, earning a research point.\n• Greenhouse (\"Closed-Loop Life Support\" technology): eight grow trays for wheat, carrots, potatoes or beetroot. With power, a crop grows in just over two minutes. Right-click to harvest, and the tray replants itself.\n• Logistics: eight cargo lockers, 216 slots in all.\n• Cupola: a glass dome to watch the planet go by. The crew also observes planets and moons from there, and each cupola (two at most) adds one to the agency observatory's power.\n• Airlock: two hatch doors. They open as you approach. The airlock equalises (air hisses, the light turns amber), the hatch rises and the light turns green, then it comes back down once the way is clear. The two doors are interlocked. While one is open, the other stays shut, and the station keeps its air.\n• Power truss (\"Large Solar Arrays\" technology): a lattice truss, two wings of four panels and a battery, outside.\nAn extra core module makes an empty room.\n\nAll these blocks can also be placed one by one. Hull, lining, floor, ceiling lights, windows and closed ports are airtight. Air passes through grow trays and the lattice truss.\n\nIf you die in space or on another world, you wake up on the planet, at your bed or the world spawn.\n\nYour agency's first station earns 250 research points, and its first experiment 50.");

