        add("capsule.orbitalis.hud.moon_hint", "Landed on the Moon · hold the launch key for 3 s to lift off");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.hud.moon_liftoff", "Lifting off from the Moon in %s s...");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.moon_liftoff", "Liftoff from the Moon: heading back to the planet's orbit.");
        add("capsule.orbitalis.hud.lunar_ascent", "Ascent: %s m");
        add("space.orbitalis.first_moonwalk", "First steps on the Moon: +%s research points.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.moon.title", "The Moon");
        add("guide.orbitalis.moon.text", "Going to the Moon takes three things.\n\nFirst, a lunar ship, a 6 t payload (\"Lunar Lander\" technology): a crew capsule sitting on its landing stage. It goes under the escape tower, like a capsule.\n\nThen a heavy launcher that can reach the Moon, which means 12,550 m/s of delta-v. Small tanks won't do. You need the staged-combustion engine, twice as powerful as the turbopump engine, and the large composite tanks, four times bigger than the others. A launcher like that weighs several hundred tonnes, so plan for a 9×9 pad with its 38-floor tower.\n\nLast, a spacesuit, because there's no air on the Moon.\n\nAfter liftoff, you stay in free orbit. On the way out, a lunar ship doesn't dock with your station, it's only passing through. Press the departure key (Sprint by default) and the flight computer plans the trip to the Moon. At the mark, hold it and let go between 95 and 110% of the delta-v. The ship arrives above the surface and comes down on its engine, slower and slower, until it touches down. No parachutes here, without air they'd be useless.\n\nOn the Moon, sneak to get out. Gravity is only a sixth of the planet's, so you jump very high and fall slowly. The ground is regolith over moon rock, peppered with craters. Bringing samples back earns research points.\n\nMoon rock contains ilmenite, which gives raw titanium, far more common here than on the planet. At the bottom of some large craters, there's still lunar ice. With a bucket, it gives water, which the electrolyzer turns into hydrogen and oxygen. Enough to make propellant on the spot.\n\nAnything that works in a station works here too. Build a closed room (out of station hull, or any solid block), put an air generator in it and power it. You can breathe there without a suit, and it recharges. Solar panels give full power, as in orbit. Close the base with two doors in a row, opening only one at a time, to keep the air in. The planet hangs in the sky.\n\nTo come back, get back on board and hold the launch key (Jump by default) for three seconds. The ship lifts off and returns to the planet's orbit, or to your station. From there, you go home as usual, under parachutes.\n\nThe first Moon landing earns 500 research points, and the first steps 300. Don't lose sight of your ship, it's your only way home.\n\nBefore sending a crew, you can send robots. A lunar or Mars probe (\"Robotic Probes\" technology) launches like a satellite. If the launcher reaches the body, it goes into orbit and maps it. A robotic lander (\"Landers\" technology) actually sets down on the surface, about thirty blocks east of the point above the pad. Its coordinates are announced, and a crew can go and visit it. The first mission of each type to each body earns a big bonus (lunar probe 60, Mars probe 100, lunar lander 100, Mars lander 150), later ones a bit of science.\n\nWith the \"In-Situ Resources\" technology, the ice drill pulls water from lunar ice, placed on it or up to four blocks above. On Mars, the oxygen extractor pulls oxygen from the atmosphere. Both have their own generator and push what they make into the tanks and pipes touching them. The electrolyzer does the rest. Right-click to see their status. With the \"Planetary Bases\" technology, the planetary base kit builds, with a right-click on the ground of the Moon or Mars, a pressurised module (the same as in a station) with its airlock door. Module kits connect to it as in orbit. The first base on each body earns 80 points.\n\nWith the \"Sample Return\" technology, a lunar or Mars sample return mission launches like a probe. It lands, scoops up soil and sends its capsule back to Earth (Moon: 120 points the first time, then 25; Mars: 200, then 40). A crew can also do the job themselves. On site, right-click the ground with a sample container to take a bit of rock. Back on Earth, a right-click hands them to the agency lab (Moon: 150 points the first time, then 30; Mars: 250, then 50).\n\nWith the \"Lunar Orbit Station\" technology, the lunar station launches like a satellite, on a launcher that can reach the Moon. It settles into lunar orbit, shows up at the tracking centre and earns 10 points a day for 300 days, plus 150 points the first time.");

