        add("guide.orbitalis.mars.title", "Mars");
        add("guide.orbitalis.mars.text", "Mars is further than the Moon, and tougher.\n\nThe Mars ship is an 8 t payload (\"Mars Lander\" technology). It's a beefed-up lunar ship, with an entry shield, big tanks and engines. It goes under the escape tower, like a capsule.\n\nYou need a heavy launcher that can reach the Mars transfer, which means 13,000 m/s of delta-v. That's a bit more than for the Moon, with a heavier payload.\n\nIn orbit, press the departure key (Sprint by default) to plan the burn. At the mark, hold it and let go between 95 and 110% of the delta-v. The ship arrives high above Mars and enters its atmosphere. It's a hundred times thinner than the planet's, enough to slow the ship but not to land it.\n\nThe landing is up to you. At 170 m above the ground, the descent engine arms. Only two forces are at play: the engine's thrust, along the ship's axis, as long as you hold the step key (Jump by default), and gravity. Engine off, the ship falls. On, it brakes. Your attitude controls tilt it, and tilted, it slides sideways. The engine throttles itself. It brakes hard as long as you're coming down too fast, then holds a steady descent, without ever pushing you back up. Keeping it on the whole way gets you down, and cutting it at times saves propellant, which is limited.\n\nFrom your seat, you can't see the ground under the ship, so land on instruments. The panel shows height, descent speed, drift, tilt and propellant. Below the attitude ball, a prompt tells you what to do: burn, let it fall, level out or cancel drift. The outside view (F5) helps too.\n\nAt touchdown, don't go over 6 m/s down, 5 m/s sideways or 25° of tilt. Beyond that, the ship breaks apart. In automatic mode, the flight computer lands for you.\n\nOn Mars, sneak to get out, in a suit. Gravity is 38% of the planet's. The ground is rust-coloured dust over brown rock, pitted with craters, under an ochre sky. The rock holds hematite, which gives iron. Bringing samples back earns research points, and the first landing is worth 900.\n\nTo come back, hold the launch key for three seconds on board. The ship lifts off and returns to the planet's orbit, from where you go home as usual.\n\nBeyond Mars, it's probes that explore. Each launches like a satellite, on a launcher that can reach its target: Venus (12,900 m/s), Jupiter (16,000), Saturn (17,200), Titan (17,500), Europa (18,500) and Mercury (22,000). Chemical engines are barely enough anymore, you need an ion stage, a sail or a nuclear engine. Orbital probes (Venus, Mercury, Jupiter, Saturn, Europa) map their body. The Venus lander and the Jupiter and Titan descent probes dive into the atmosphere and measure it all the way down. Distant probes draw their power from a reactor core (\"Radioisotope Generators\" technology). The first mission of each type to each body earns a big bonus, bigger the further away it is.");
        add("block.orbitalis.moon_ilmenite_ore", "Lunar Ilmenite Ore");
        add("block.orbitalis.lunar_ice", "Lunar Ice");

        // Manuel : tailles de pas de tir et missions
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_sizes.title", "The Three Launch Pads");
        add("guide.orbitalis.missions.title", "Which Pad for Which Mission");
        add("guide.orbitalis.missions.text", "Each mission has its own needs, in pad size, launcher power, and sometimes propellants or equipment. The design computer's catalogue has a launcher for each.\n\nSounding rocket (suborbital flight)\n- 3×3 test pad, or 5×5 pad.\n- A solid motor, a sounding rocket payload and a fairing.\n- No propellant to load, the solid motor has its own.\n- It goes above 100 km and earns 5 research points per flight, plus the first-flight bonuses.\n\nLiquid-fuel test\n- 5×5 pad, with tanks hooked up at the foot of the tower.\n- Two pressure-fed engines and one tank.\n- RP-1 and liquid oxygen.\n- A chance to get fuelling right before aiming higher.\n\nSatellite in low or polar orbit (experimental, weather, observation, telescope)\n- 5×5 pad for a 125 t launcher. The telescope, at 11 t, needs a heavier one.\n- Two stages and 9,400 m/s of delta-v.\n- RP-1 and liquid oxygen.\n- On the ground, a tracking station to follow the satellite and receive its data.\n\nSatellite in medium or geostationary orbit (navigation, telecommunications)\n- 5×5 or 9×9 pad, depending on the launcher.\n- 11,850 m/s of delta-v, so a cryogenic upper stage.\n- RP-1, liquid oxygen and liquid hydrogen.\n- With too weak a launcher, the satellite is lost. The table warns you when you set the rocket up.\n\nCrewed suborbital flight\n- 5×5 pad.\n- A crew capsule on a single stage.\n- Board through the boarding gate and its walkway, or by right-clicking the cabin. Launch from the cabin (jump key) or from the console.\n- The capsule comes back under parachutes, a little way from the pad.\n\nCrewed orbital flight\n- 5×5 pad.\n- Two stages, 9,400 m/s with the 2.8 t capsule.\n- A spacesuit to get out of the capsule.\n- In orbit, the jump key prepares the return.\n\nSpace station\n- 5×5 pad.\n- Two stages, 9,400 m/s with the 3 t core module, launched uncrewed.\n- A crewed orbital flight from the same pad then docks with it, and more modules extend it.\n- The station deploys right above the pad, so always launch to it from that site.\n\nLunar flight\n- 9×9 pad, 38-floor tower.\n- Three stages, 12,550 m/s with the 6 t lunar ship. You need staged-combustion engines and large composite tanks. The catalogue example weighs 355 t and stands 26 blocks tall.\n- RP-1, liquid oxygen and plenty of liquid hydrogen.\n- A spacesuit.\n- In orbit, the sprint key prepares the departure for the Moon.\n\nIn every case:\n- the launch console goes 8 to 64 blocks from the table;\n- fuelling happens by itself as long as the tanks stay hooked to the fuel ports;\n- you can't launch in a thunderstorm;\n- the pad screen (right-click the table or a slab) shows the allowed mass and height, and those of the rocket on it.");

        add("capsule.orbitalis.in_orbit_lunar", "You're in orbit, ready to leave for the Moon. Departure key (Sprint by default) to plan the trip, return key (Jump) to head home. At the mark, hold it and let go between 95 and 110% of the delta-v.");

