        add("block.orbitalis.test_console", "Test Console");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.table.test_pad_crew", "The test pad has no gangway, so no crewed flights here. You need a 5×5 pad or bigger.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.table.test_pad_liquid", "The test pad has no arm to fuel a rocket. It only takes solid-fuel rockets.");
        add("sequence.orbitalis.alert.no_pad", "No launch table within %s blocks.");
        add("sequence.orbitalis.alert.pad_not_ready", "The pad isn't ready. Finish it, then right-click its table.");
        add("sequence.orbitalis.alert.no_rocket", "No rocket on the table.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.kit.tower.none", "None (test pad)");
        add("guide.orbitalis.test_pad.title", "The Test Pad");
        add("guide.orbitalis.test_pad.text", "Your first sounding rockets don't need a tower. A test pad is enough.\n\nIn the launch pad kit, pick the 3×3 size. It's a plain platform: a flame deflector under the table, supports at the corners, eight deck slabs and a flame trench. No tower, no masts, no arm.\n\nIt only takes uncrewed solid-fuel rockets, up to 20 t and 8 blocks tall. A liquid-fuel rocket needs an arm to fill its tanks, and a crew capsule needs a gangway, so those need a 5×5 pad.\n\nThe test console is crafted from 3 steel plates, 2 iron ingots, 1 glass pane, 1 circuit board and 2 redstone. Place it at least 8 blocks from the table, and no more than 64. It only shows the launch sequence, with no map and no rocket sheet.\n\nOnce the pad is in service, build your rocket on it, open the console and work down the list: the Go/No-Go poll if you want one, ignition, then release the clamps when thrust reaches 100%%.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.kit.use.2", "From sounding rockets to two-stage orbital launchers: tests, satellites, crewed flights, station modules.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.kit.use.3", "Heavy launchers: lunar flight, large satellites to high orbit.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.kit.use.4", "Heavy launchers: lunar flight, Mars flight, large satellites to high orbit.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.kit.use.5", "The largest launchers there are.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.elevator.hint", "In the cabin: right-click the floor to choose the level");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.text", "A launch pad is built from a launch pad kit, made of a launch table and a sheet of paper.\n\nRight-clicking with the kit opens its screen. You choose the deck size (5×5, 9×9 or 11×11) and the tower, with stairs or an elevator. The screen shows the mass and height of the rockets this pad can launch, the space it takes, and the blocks you need next to what you already have. Once confirmed, the launch table is placed one block above the ground.\n\nThe blueprint appears in the world right away, each block in place as a see-through ghost. The layers in progress are detailed and outlined in yellow, the rest is just a silhouette. You can place blocks one by one, or right-click the table (or any deck slab) with nothing in hand to place the whole yellow layer at once with what you're carrying. Block orientation doesn't matter, the table fixes it. Right-clicking the table tells you what's still missing.\n\nUnder the deck sit the flame deflector and the supports. On top come the slab deck with its flame trench, a mast on each corner, the deluge rails, and the five-block-wide tower on the side opposite the trench. The tower starts from the ground. Its utility column, in the middle of the side facing the rocket, carries the umbilical arms, and a railed platform caps it. All these blocks are solid, so you can walk on them.\n\nIn the stair tower, you go in through a side door and a spiral staircase climbs around the core to the top, one side per floor. Nothing overhangs it closer than four blocks.\n\nIn the elevator tower, you go in from the back into a car that fills the whole inside, three by three and three high. Right-click the car floor to open the floor panel. It stops at the ground and at every boarding gate. Right-clicking a boarding gate calls it. The shaft is enclosed, and the landing doors only open when the car is there.\n\nA mast floodlight lights up to sixteen blocks ahead of it, but not its surroundings, and anything in the way cuts the beam. On a working pad, the floodlights turn toward the rocket and come on at night and during launches, and the mast beacons blink. Anywhere else, a floodlight is switched on by redstone.\n\nCareful: from ignition until the rocket is high up, the exhaust burns everything at the foot of the rocket, on the deck and far along the trench. Keep your distance.\n\nA two-flight staircase on the left side of the deck leads up from the ground.\n\nOnce the last block is placed and the sky above the table is clear, the blueprint disappears. Right-click the table to form the pad, provided your agency has the \"Launch Pad\" technology. The deck, masts and arms take on their finished look. If a block gets broken, the pad comes apart at once and the blueprint returns to show what's missing.\n\nFor fuelling, swap a frame block at the foot of the tower for a fuel port (\"Tank Farm\" technology). Right-click it to choose its propellant: kerosene, liquid oxygen or liquid hydrogen. You need one per propellant the rocket carries. Put your tanks against it or connect them with a pipe. As soon as a rocket is on the table, fuelling happens by itself, kerosene first, then the cold propellants, which slowly boil off while the rocket waits.\n\nThe boarding gate, three blocks wide and tall, opens in the utility column, facing the rocket. It's part of the blueprint, like the umbilical arm, so place it where the blueprint shows. As soon as a crewed rocket, or its blueprint, is on the table, the table moves the boarding gate up to the cabin's height. The staircase turns with it so that a landing always opens onto it. A walkway reaches from the arm to the rocket, and retracts fifteen seconds before liftoff.\n\nFor mission control, place a launch console 8 to 64 blocks from the table. Control screens (\"Mission Control\" technology) placed side by side form a big screen from three wide and two high. To link it, sneak and right-click the console, then right-click the screen wall.\n\nWith a tower block or a mast segment in hand, right-clicking a column you've already started places the block on top, like scaffolding.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_sizes.text", "There are three pad sizes. The bigger the deck, the heavier the rocket can be, and the taller the tower, the taller the rocket can be, since it has to stay below the top. They're all built the same way (see \"Launch Complex\"), and the kit gives the exact block list for each size.\n\nThe 5×5 launches rockets up to 200 t and 24 blocks tall. Its tower has 26 floors and it covers 10×5 blocks. It's the starter pad, for sounding rockets, satellites and the first crewed flights.\nThe 9×9 goes up to 3,000 t and 36 blocks, with a 38-floor tower, on 14×9 blocks. It's the heavy launcher pad, for the Moon, Mars and high orbits.\nThe 11×11 goes up to 10,000 t and 44 blocks, with a 46-floor tower, on 16×11 blocks.\n\nTo change size or tower, right-click the table with another kit. The blueprint is replaced and shows what needs adding. There must be nothing above the table.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.1", "Right-click with the kit, then pick the size and the tower. The pad is previewed where you're looking, and the scroll wheel rotates it. Right-clicking the ground places the launch table one block up, and the blueprint appears.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.2", "Under the deck, the flame deflector goes below the table, surrounded by the supports that will carry the slabs.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.3", "The deck goes in at table level. Leave the flame trench line clear.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.4", "The flame trench is a straight line of gratings from the table to the edge. The table turns them the right way by itself.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.5", "A lightning mast stands on each corner, with a floodlight just below its top. The two deluge rails frame the rocket, open in the middle to leave room for the boosters.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.6", "Start the tower with its utility column. It rises from the ground, against the deck, in the middle of the side facing the rocket.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.7", "Then the rest of the tower, five blocks wide: the frame, the stairs and landings (or the elevator shaft), and a railed platform on top.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.8", "The umbilical arm hangs from the utility column on the rocket side, and the boarding gate opens in the same face. The blueprint shows where they go.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.9", "With the last block in place, the blueprint disappears. Right-click the table to form the pad. If a block gets broken, the blueprint comes back to show what's missing.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.launch_complex.scene.10", "Swap a frame block at the foot of the tower for a fuel port, one per propellant, and hook your tanks to it. The console must be at least 8 blocks from the table.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.1", "Right-click with the kit, then pick the size and the tower. The pad is previewed where you're looking, and the scroll wheel rotates it. Right-clicking the ground places the launch table one block up, and the blueprint appears.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.2", "Under the deck, the flame deflector goes below the table, surrounded by the supports that will carry the slabs.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.3", "The deck goes in at table level. Leave the flame trench line clear.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.4", "The flame trench is a straight line of gratings from the table to the edge. The table turns them the right way by itself.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.5", "A lightning mast stands on each corner, with a floodlight just below its top. The two deluge rails frame the rocket, open in the middle to leave room for the boosters.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.6", "Start the tower with its utility column. It rises from the ground, against the deck, in the middle of the side facing the rocket.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.7", "Then the rest of the tower, five blocks wide: the frame, the stairs and landings (or the elevator shaft), and a railed platform on top.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.8", "The umbilical arm hangs from the utility column on the rocket side, and the boarding gate opens in the same face. The blueprint shows where they go.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.9", "With the last block in place, the blueprint disappears. Right-click the table to form the pad. If a block gets broken, the blueprint comes back to show what's missing.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.scene.10", "Swap a frame block at the foot of the tower for a fuel port, one per propellant, and hook your tanks to it. The console must be at least 8 blocks from the table.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.title", "9×9 Launch Pad");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_9x9.text", "The 9×9 launch pad takes rockets up to 3,000 t and 36 blocks tall, beyond a thousand tonnes.\n\nIts tower has 38 floors, and the whole thing covers 14×9 blocks, deck included.\n\nThe demo builds it piece by piece, in order. Step through it, rotate it and zoom with the scroll wheel. It shows an elevator tower, but the stair tower is built the same way.\n\nThe exact block list is in the kit's screen. It depends on the size and the tower, and counts what you already have.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.1", "Right-click with the kit, then pick the size and the tower. The pad is previewed where you're looking, and the scroll wheel rotates it. Right-clicking the ground places the launch table one block up, and the blueprint appears.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.2", "Under the deck, the flame deflector goes below the table, surrounded by the supports that will carry the slabs.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.3", "The deck goes in at table level. Leave the flame trench line clear.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.4", "The flame trench is a straight line of gratings from the table to the edge. The table turns them the right way by itself.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.5", "A lightning mast stands on each corner, with a floodlight just below its top. The two deluge rails frame the rocket, open in the middle to leave room for the boosters.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.6", "Start the tower with its utility column. It rises from the ground, against the deck, in the middle of the side facing the rocket.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.7", "Then the rest of the tower, five blocks wide: the frame, the stairs and landings (or the elevator shaft), and a railed platform on top.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.8", "The umbilical arm hangs from the utility column on the rocket side, and the boarding gate opens in the same face. The blueprint shows where they go.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.9", "With the last block in place, the blueprint disappears. Right-click the table to form the pad. If a block gets broken, the blueprint comes back to show what's missing.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.scene.10", "Swap a frame block at the foot of the tower for a fuel port, one per propellant, and hook your tanks to it. The console must be at least 8 blocks from the table.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.title", "11×11 Launch Pad");
        add("guide.orbitalis.pad_11x11.text", "The 11×11 launch pad takes rockets up to 10,000 t and 44 blocks tall, the biggest launchers in the game.\n\nIts tower has 46 floors, and the whole thing covers 16×11 blocks, deck included.\n\nThe demo builds it piece by piece, in order. Step through it, rotate it and zoom with the scroll wheel. It shows an elevator tower, but the stair tower is built the same way.\n\nThe exact block list is in the kit's screen. It depends on the size and the tower, and counts what you already have.");

