        add("guide.orbitalis.titan.title", "Titan");
        add("guide.orbitalis.titan.text", "Titan, Saturn's big moon, is the furthest place you can land.\n\nThe Titan ship is a 7 t payload (\"Titan Lander\" technology, which needs the Mars lander and nuclear thermal propulsion). It's a four-seat Mk3 cabin inside an entry shell, with parachutes and a small ascent stage. It goes under the escape tower, like a capsule.\n\nYou need a very heavy launcher that can reach Titan, which means 17,500 m/s of delta-v. Chemical engines aren't enough. You need a nuclear thermal upper stage, which burns only liquid hydrogen, with its big, light hydrogen tanks. For example, three staged-combustion engines and sixteen composite tanks, then a cryogenic engine and twelve composite tanks, then a nuclear engine and twenty-four hydrogen tanks. Ion engines and sails won't do for a crewed flight, they push too little.\n\nIn orbit, press the departure key (Sprint by default) to plan the burn. At the mark, hold it and let go between 95 and 110% of the delta-v. The ship arrives high above Titan and goes in shield first. Titan's air is four times denser than the planet's. It slows the ship enough for its parachutes to land it, like the Huygens probe in 2005. Arm them as you would coming home (in automatic mode, the flight computer handles it). Without them, the ship crashes.\n\nOn Titan, sneak to get out, in a suit. You can't breathe there, the air is nitrogen and methane at -179 °C. Gravity is 14% of the planet's, so you jump very high and falls barely hurt. The ground is brown tholin sand, organic grains fallen from the haze, over water ice as hard as rock. Under the orange haze, you can't see far.\n\nBelow a certain height, the low ground is full of liquid methane. You can swim and sail in it like water, and scoop methane with a bucket. You can also drown in it, and you only see a few blocks. Anywhere but Titan, poured methane boils away at once. Water, on the other hand, freezes on the spot, so you can't pour it.\n\nTo come back, hold the launch key for three seconds on board. The ship lifts off and returns to the planet's orbit, from where you go home as usual.\n\nThe first landing on Titan earns 1,200 research points, and the first steps 700. The planetary base kit works on Titan too.\n\nThe flying drone (\"Rotorcraft\" technology) sits on the ground, and a right-click sends it to survey a site up to a hundred blocks away. It comes back on its own, recharges its battery for thirty seconds, then sets off again. Each new site earns 25 research points, and the first flight 150. On Mars, the air is so thin that it only makes forty-block hops, for 15 points.");
