        add("guide.orbitalis.methane.title", "Methane");
        add("guide.orbitalis.methane.text", "Methalox is liquid methane burned with liquid oxygen, as in Raptor engines. Denser than hydrogen and cleaner than kerosene, its big advantage is that it can be made far from the planet.\n\nIt needs the \"Methane / Oxygen Engines\" technology, which unlocks the methalox engine and the Sabatier reactor.\n\nOn the planet, it takes two steps:\n1. The Sabatier reactor takes coal (or charcoal) and gaseous hydrogen. 800 mB of hydrogen and one coal give 400 mB of methane and 200 mB of water. Methane comes out on the left, water on the right.\n2. The cryogenic liquefier turns it into liquid methane, 50 mB of liquid for 100 mB of gas.\n\nOn Titan, place the ice drill at the edge of a methane lake. It pumps liquid methane from every lake block within reach and pushes it into the tanks touching it. A bucket works too.\n\nAt the pad, a methalox rocket needs a methane fuel port, in the back corner at the foot of the tower, in addition to the oxygen one. Like oxygen, methane is loaded after kerosene.\n\nLiquid methane is cryogenic and boils off outside a cooled tank, except on Titan, where it's -179 °C.");
