        add("guide.orbitalis.europa.title", "Europa");
        add("guide.orbitalis.europa.text", "Europa, Jupiter's icy moon, hides under its ice shell a salty ocean bigger than all of the planet's put together. It's the best place in the solar system to look for life.\n\nTo get there, you need:\n- the Europa ship (\"Europa Lander\" technology), a Mk3 cabin on a descent stage, under armour;\n- a launcher that can reach Europa, which means 18,500 m/s. It needs a nuclear stage and its hydrogen tanks, for example four staged-combustion engines and twenty-four composite tanks, then a cryogenic engine and sixteen composite tanks, then a nuclear engine and thirty-two hydrogen tanks;\n- an advanced suit with its radiation shielding module.\n\nIn orbit, press the departure key (Sprint by default) to plan the burn. At the mark, hold it and let go between 95 and 110% of the delta-v. Europa has no air, so the ship lands on its engine, like on the Moon. To leave, hold the launch key.\n\nEuropa is a shell of bluish ice, almost flat, streaked with long red-brown bands (the lineae, salts pushed up from the ocean) between ice ridges, with patches of jumbled, broken plates here and there. Gravity is 13% of the planet's, it's -160 °C, there's no air, and Jupiter fills a good part of the sky.\n\nJupiter bathes Europa in deadly particles. Without the shielding module on the suit's chest, you lose health every second you spend outside. The ship and bases protect you.\n\nThe cryobot (\"Cryobots\" technology) sits on the ice. Its heated head melts through the shell, and it goes down twenty kilometres to the ocean in ten minutes. Right-click it to see how far along it is. The first time an agency reaches the ocean, it finds traces of life. It's the biggest discovery in the game, worth 2,000 research points. Later dives earn 100.\n\nThe first landing earns 1,500 points, and the first steps 800. The planetary base kit works on Europa too.");
