        add("design.orbitalis.rocket.heavy.desc", "Three stages, staged combustion then hydrogen: high orbits and lunar flight.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.rocket.plan_placed", "%s laid: %s blocks to place above the table, one by one.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.rocket.plan_taken", "Rocket blueprint taken back.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.rocket.formed", "Rocket assembled: it is ready to be fuelled.");
        add("spaceport.orbitalis.rocket.unformed", "Rocket taken apart: its blocks are back in place.");
        add("guide.orbitalis.rocket_assembly.title", "Design and Build");
        add("guide.orbitalis.rocket_assembly.text", "A rocket is drawn up at the design computer, then built block by block on its pad.\n\nThe design computer is a big console, three blocks wide and two high. Right-click to open it. The rocket is shown in 3D on the left, the stage editor on the right. Start from a known model (from the sounding rocket to the Mars launcher) or a blank sheet, pick the payload, then set up to three stages. For each one, you choose the engine and how many, then the tank type and how many. Engines that carry their own propellant (solid, hypergolic, ion) have no tank. Each stage shows its delta-v and thrust-to-weight ratio, and the whole rocket its mass, delta-v, propellants, destination and first problem. Only parts studied at the engineer's workbench are offered. The scroll wheel steps through a choice or a number.\n\n\"Print blueprint\" gives you a rocket blueprint. It costs a sheet of paper and the chosen payload, which you must be carrying and which goes with the blueprint. Your agency must master every part of the launcher.\n\nWith the blueprint in hand, right-click the table of a formed pad that's big enough. The blocks to place appear above the table as models, layer by layer. Right-clicking the table tells you what's missing.\n\nEach section has its block, in the middle. Parts place themselves. Right-click the matching model with an engine, a tank, an interstage or a nose cone, and the part slots in. Each block costs what the blueprint asks. The bay of a three-engine stage takes three, a tank section of a big rocket takes two, and on a slim rocket one tank spans two blocks (the second is free, but you need the part in hand). A removed block gives back what it cost. The payload bay, fairing, hull panels, cable raceway, flight computer, solid motor casing, cabin, roof and escape tower are crafted and placed like ordinary blocks.\n\nWhen a rocket is waiting to be built, right-clicking the table opens the build screen. It shows the rocket as it stands, the placed blocks, the current layer, then what's left to gather piece by piece next to what you're carrying, any missing fuelling hookups and the payload on board. \"Place layer\" places the next layer, and \"Place all\" everything you can.\n\nIn the editor, the first stage can carry a pair of solid boosters (\"Solid Boosters\" technology). They burn with it at liftoff, add their thrust and delta-v, and drop off on their own once empty, with no step to command. On the pad, they stand on either side of the rocket, at right angles to the tower, up to seven tenths of the first stage's height. Each is built from its nozzle (the booster itself), solid motor casings and a nose cone.\n\nOnce the rocket is standing, the pad screen offers \"Copy blueprint\". Like printing, the copy costs a sheet of paper and the payload. It's handy for keeping a hand-built rocket and building it again elsewhere.\n\nA heavy launcher is three blocks wide. Each central block is ringed with hull panels, with a cable raceway on the tower side, the extra engines around the first one, and the flight computer at the top of the last stage.\n\nThe rocket forms when the last block goes in. If you break one of its sections, it comes apart. Its blocks come back, except the one you broke, which drops to the ground, and the blueprint shows where to put it back. Sneak and right-click the table with an empty hand to take the rocket apart, and once it's dismantled, the same move takes the blueprint back.\n\nAfter launch, the blueprint stays on the pad. You can build the same rocket again, or take it back to place another.\n\nYou can also build without a blueprint. On a working pad, stack an engine and its tanks along the table's axis, maybe an interstage and another stage, then finish with a payload bay and a nose cone, or a cabin with its roof and tower. With each block, the table shows what the rocket is worth (delta-v, destination, mass) and what it's missing. The more tanks a stage has, the more propellant it carries. Hand the payload to the table with a right-click. Once the stack makes a rocket, the table draws up a blueprint from it, and all that's left is to place what it shows, like the hull of a wide rocket or the fuel ports. Sneak with an empty hand to take that blueprint, and your invention can then be rebuilt like any printed one.");
        add("block.orbitalis.tower_deck", "Tower Deck Slab");
